Rainbow tables are a specialized type of hashing attack where you query database logs, social media profiles, and public records to generate millions of unique, similarly-shaped bit strings. In this context, building a full key-generation suite on an Arduino is less about "keys," and more about managing deployment risks. These attacks target credential hashes by probing numerous hashes simultaneously; a single leak can cascade across millions of accounts. Any approach built on an "Arduino" or "neighbour" environment must still respect hardware constraints and attention to physical security to prevent inadvertent compromise of infrastructure.

