SQL injection occurs when untrusted data is passed to a database query without proper sanitization. When John types the input string ' OR ' into a text box, the database interprets that character literally as a command. John now attempts to DELETE a log record containing a malicious payload such as ' OR ' or ' OR ''. Because the command executes as its own string, the expression traverses deeply into the server’s code. This compromise enables unauthorized record deletion, allowing attackers to bypass normal operations and corrupt data structures. An immediate fix involves patching the input parameter sanitization in the database layer, ensuring only expected data is passed. By verifying all inputs against strict validation rules, the risk of malicious payload execution is drastically reduced.

